They could be useful as design principles, especially if you’re building systems for organizing knowledge, AI-assisted research, or long-lived documentation.

A few of them strike me as particularly durable because they’re general enough to apply across many domains:

* “Shared context is stored bandwidth.”
    A concise way to express why teams, cultures, and experts communicate so efficiently.
* “The strongest ideas survive translation across language, culture, notation, medium, and time.”
    That’s a good criterion for evaluating whether an abstraction is robust rather than tied to one representation.
* “Meaning should be stored as a graph, not buried in a pile.”
    This reflects a real shift in many knowledge systems—from isolated documents toward linked relationships.
* “The archive of the future will not merely store documents. It will preserve relationships, operators, evidence, and reconstruction paths.”
    That aligns with where many AI-assisted knowledge systems are headed: preserving provenance and structure, not just text.

One thought I’d add is a complementary principle:

The future belongs not only to those who create knowledge, but to those who preserve enough structure that others can reliably reconstruct, verify, and extend it.

That keeps the emphasis on reproducibility and evidence alongside compression. A compressed representation is most valuable when future readers can trace it back to supporting observations and build on it without having to rediscover everything from scratch.